<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drobeta-Turnu Severin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Turnu Severin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drobeta-Turnu Severin is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, just below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a> gorge. On the site of the Roman town of Drobeta, where Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s bridge across the Danube in 105, a medieval fortress, Severin, was raised, and it was the seat of the Hungarian Banate of Severin from the 13th century. The town later became part of Wallachia and grew as a Danube port in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drobeta-turnu-severin.jpg" alt="Drobeta-Turnu Severin" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boxenhorst · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cultural-Palace-Teodor-Costescu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Drobeta-Turnu Severin lies on the northern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a>, in Mehedinți County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>. Drobeta is the name of the ancient Dacian and Roman towns at the site, first mentioned by Ptolemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the 2nd century CE. Turnu means tower, after a tower built on the north bank by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and Severin is variously traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, to an Old Church Slavonic word for north, or to Severinus of Noricum, patron saint of the medieval colony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Bridge was built here in three years, between 103 and 105 CE, by Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>. It was considered one of the most daring works of the Roman world, with twenty arches between stone piers. Drobeta became the third urban centre of Dacia, after Sarmizegetusa and Apullum. Hadrian made it a municipium in 121, and in 193 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> raised it to a colony, giving its residents equal rights with Roman citizens. By the middle of the 3rd century it had almost 40,000 inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> destroyed it in the 5th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> rebuilt it.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Hungarian crown organised the Banate of Severin; its first Ban, Luca, is mentioned in 1233, and a new castle, Severin, rose over the ruins of Drobeta. The fortress was the most important strategic redoubt on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and the Wallachian rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litovoi">Litovoi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basarab-i">Basarab I</a> died there. In 1524 a devastating attack by the Turks led by Suleiman left only one tower standing, which gave the town its name, the Tower of Severinus. It remained under Ottoman rule until 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the Treaty of Adrianople in 1829 the city was rebuilt from 1836, and the harbour was built in 1858. Severin became the county capital in 1841 and a city in 1851. By 1900 it had railway workshops and a shipyard that by 1914 was the largest in the country. In 1972 the name Drobeta was added to the city's name, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>'s rule. The remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Bridge, the bridgehead fort, the Roman baths and the amphitheatre are visible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drobeta-Turnu Severin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Andrew II's son Béla set up the banate around 1228–1233, in lands taken from Bulgaria and the Cumans east of the Iron Gates, around the fortress of <b>Severin</b> (Drobeta-Turnu Severin) built on Roman ruins.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-severin">Banate of Severin</a></li></ul></section>
